     "text": "Certainly! Based on the coding standards and best practices emphasized by your mentor Luis, here is a **Linked List class** implementation in Python. This implementation adheres to the following principles:\n\n- **Consistent attribute naming** (ends with `_at` as per the latest convention).\n- **Clear and concise comments** to ensure readability and maintainability.\n- **Error handling** using `try` and `assert` statements.\n- **Modular design** with well-defined methods.\n- **Documentation annotations** for method parameters and return values.\n\n```python\nclass LinkedList:\n    \"\"\"\n    A linked list data structure that allows for efficient insertion and deletion\n    of elements at the beginning and end of the list.\n\n    Attributes:\n        head (Node): The first node in the linked list.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes an empty linked list with no nodes.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.head = None  # _at: head of the linked list\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns a string representation of the linked list.\n\n        Returns:\n            str: A string containing the elements of the linked list, space-separated.\n        \"\"\"\n        result = []\n        current = self.head\n        while current:\n            result.append(str(current.data))\n            current = current._next  # _at: next node in the list\n        return ' '.join(result)\n\n    def append(self, data):\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new node with the given data to the end of the linked list.\n\n        Args:\n            data: The data to be stored in the new node.\n        \"\"\"\n        new_node = Node(data)\n        if not self.head:\n            self.head = new_node\n        else:\n            current = self.head\n            while current._next:  # _at: next node in the list\n                current = current._next\n            current._next = new_node\n\n    def prepend(self, data):\n        \"\"\"\n        Adds a new node with the given data to the beginning of the linked list.\n\n        Args:\n            data: The data to be stored in the new node.\n        \"\"\"\n        new_node = Node(data)\n        new_node._next = self.head  # _at: next node in the list\n        self.head = new_node\n\n    def delete(self, data):\n        \"\"\"\n        Deletes the first occurrence of a node with the given data from the linked list.\n\n        Args:\n            data: The data to be removed from the linked list.\n\n        Raises:\n            ValueError:",
